 ],
 "literal": "Of the Potiyil mountain, difficult to approach and unknown to living beings, / resembling the sandalwood on the mountain slopes possessed by spirits, / she is cool in the summer; and in the winter, / gently closing as the withdrawing rays of the sun gather, / like a lotus that has enclosed the shaking sunshine / within its interior, she has a slight warmth.",
 "translation": "Like the sandalwood on the spirit-haunted slopes of the Potiyil mountain, difficult to approach and unknown to living beings, she is cool in the summer. In the winter, she has a gentle warmth, like the interior of a lotus that has closed, trapping the shaking rays of the retreating sun.",
 "modern": "உயிரினங்கள் அறியாத, நெருங்குவதற்கு அரிய பொதிய மலையின், அணங்குகள் வாழும் பக்க மலைகளில் விளையும் சந்தனத்தைப் போல, அவள் கோடைக் காலத்தில் குளிர்ச்சியானவள். பனிக் காலத்தில், மறையும் கதிரவனின் கதிர்களைத் திரட்டி மெல்லக் குவிந்து, அசையும் வெயிலைத் தன்னுள் அடக்கிக் கொண்ட தாமரை மலரின் உட்பகுதியைப் போல, அவள் மெல்லிய வெம்மை உடையவள்.",
 "explanation": "The hero praises the physical nature of his beloved, describing how her touch adapts perfectly to the seasons. In the hot summer, she is as cool as the rare sandalwood from the sacred, inaccessible slopes of the Potiyil mountain. In the cold winter, she offers a gentle, comforting warmth, which he compares to the inside of a lotus flower that has closed at sunset, trapping the last warm rays of the sun. This beautiful comparison highlights her comforting presence and the deep intimacy they share. Although the colophon places this poem under Neytal tiṇai, the imagery of mountains and lotuses beautifully conveys the hero's deep longing and appreciation of his beloved's touch.",
